  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis highlight the mid/upper \nridge sliding eastward off the Four Corners as an upper shortwave \nbegins to enter the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, high pressure \nhas shifted toward the Mississippi River as lee troughing commences \nacross the Northern High Plains. Gusty south winds arising from the \nresultant tightening surface pressure gradient have been observed \nacross portions of central Kansas so far today. Most of the \narea should remain dry through the overnight hours and beyond, \nthough a handful of light showers may find their way into \ncentral Kansas early Saturday morning per the latest short-term \nguidance. These should be low-impact if they manage to hold \ntogether, and silent PoPs were maintained along/north of I-70 \nowing to low confidence in coverage and the general ability for \nactivity to maintain itself this far east.\n\nBreezy south winds will spread to most locations through the \nweekend and persist into next week ahead of the surface trough \nexiting the High Plains. As the upper ridge continues its slide \neastward, hot temperatures will return to the forecast area with\nhighs expected to reach into the 100-105 degree range for most \nlocations Saturday and Sunday. This heat is expected to continue\nfor most of next week as we remain under the influence of the \nmid/upper high, with highs near 100 degrees forecast through at \nleast Thursday. Thanks to widespread subsidence, most of the \narea will remain dry through this period, though portions of \ncentral Kansas may get in on some isolated showers and storms \nduring the late evening and overnight hours on Monday and \nTuesday as the surface boundary slowly shifts to the east. \nBetter chances look to reside to our northwest, however, so we \nare not expecting much in the way of meaningful rainfall through\nat least the middle of next week and likely beyond.",
